# Page Structure & Formatting

This guide explains how to structure a wiki page, add its metadata, include
images and links, format its content, and place it in the sidebar.

If you are new to Git or pull requests, read [Git Practices](/contribute/git-practices)
alongside this page.

:::note

You do not need to understand Svelte to write most pages. Markdown and a small
amount of MDsveX syntax are enough for almost all contributions.

:::

## Where Pages Live

Every page is a `+page.svx` file inside `src/routes`. Its folders become the
page's URL:

| Page type   | File                                                      | URL                            |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Wiki        | `src/routes/wiki/<category>/<page-slug>/+page.svx`        | `/wiki/<category>/<page-slug>` |
| Guide       | `src/routes/guide/<category>/<page-slug>/+page.svx`       | `/guide/<category>/<page-slug>` |

Use lowercase, descriptive, kebab-case folder names such as
`custom-worldgen` or `installing-a-datapack`.

For example:

```
src/
└── routes/
    └── guide/
        └── getting-started/
            └── +page.svx
```

That file is served at `/guide/getting-started`.

## Starting a Page

A page has three parts:

1. **Frontmatter** containing metadata such as its title and description.
2. **Content** written primarily in Markdown.
3. **Optional components** for features Markdown cannot provide by itself.

Use this as a starting point:

```md
---
title: "Example Page"
description:
  "A short, plain-language summary of what readers will learn on this page."
tags: guide, example
version: "26.2"
---

<script lang="ts">
  // Import only components used by this page.
  import { Discord, Highlight } from "$lib/reusables";
</script>

# Example Page

Introduce the topic and explain what the reader will learn.

## First Section

Start the page content here.
```

### Frontmatter

Frontmatter sits between the two `---` lines at the very top of the file.

- `title` is required and is used in browser and search metadata.
- `description` is required and should briefly explain what the page covers.
- `version` is optional. Use the latest Minecraft Java version against which
  the page was checked, and quote it as a string, such as `"26.2"`.
- `tags` is optional. Write it as one comma-separated line, not a YAML list.

The frontmatter title, page `# Heading`, and sidebar label should closely match.
Do not import or add the `Version` component yourself. When `version` is
present, the site automatically adds the version notice above the article.

### Page Content

Start with one `#` heading and a short introduction. Organise longer content
with `##` sections and `###` subsections. Use components only when Markdown is
not enough, and import only the components the page actually uses.

## Linking to Pages and Sections

Use site-root-relative URLs. Link to the public page URL, not its `.svx` file:

```md
[Functions](/wiki/files/functions)
[Macro functions](/wiki/files/functions#macro-functions)
[The images section](#images)
```

Headings automatically receive IDs. A heading such as `## Adding the Page to
Navigation` can be linked as `#adding-the-page-to-navigation`.

Use normal external URLs when linking to another website. External destinations
are fine; externally hosted article images are not the preferred approach.

## Images

Store images used by an article in `static/images`, grouped by the part of the
site and the page that owns them:

```
static/images/guides/<page-slug>/<filename>.png
static/images/wiki/<page-slug>/<filename>.png
```

Add another route folder when it helps avoid ambiguous names. For example:

```
static/images/guides/smithing-trims/example-template.png
```

Files inside `static` are served from the root of the website. The file above
is therefore referenced as `/images/guides/smithing-trims/example-template.png`.
Do not include `static` in the URL, use `../` paths, or link to a path on your
computer.

### File Names and Formats

- Use descriptive, lowercase, kebab-case names such as `datapack-enabled.png`.
- Avoid names such as `image.png`, spaces, and opaque upload IDs.
- Use lowercase file extensions.
- Keep screenshots at a useful resolution, but compress unnecessarily large
  files before committing them.
- Only commit an image when the project is allowed to redistribute it.

Do not hotlink embedded article images from Imgur, Postimages, or similar
services. Download an image the project is allowed to use, place it in the
appropriate `static/images` folder, and commit it with the page. If its licence
is unclear, link to the original source instead of copying it.

### Adding a Normal Image

For screenshots and ordinary illustrations, use Markdown:

```md
![The Data Packs screen with the new pack enabled](/images/guides/installing-a-datapack/datapack-enabled.png)
```

Markdown images automatically receive the site's responsive sizing, cut-out
corners, and frame. You do not need to import an image component for them.

Write alt text that explains the useful information visible in context. Avoid
phrases such as “image of” and do not use the filename as alt text. Use empty
alt text (`![](...)`) only when an image is genuinely decorative.

### Images With a Custom Width

Import `ArticleImage` only when an image needs additional properties, such as a
controlled width:

```md
<script lang="ts">
  import ArticleImage from "$lib/reusables/ArticleImage.svelte";
</script>

<ArticleImage
  src="/images/guides/smithing-trims/example-template.png"
  alt="Grayscale example texture for a humanoid smithing trim"
  width="300"
/>
```

Omit `width` for normal screenshots so they can use the available article
width. Do not set a fixed height; the shared component preserves the image's
aspect ratio.

For genuine Minecraft pixel art, add pixelated rendering:

```md
<ArticleImage
  src="/images/guides/smithing-trims/example-template.png"
  alt="Grayscale example texture for a humanoid smithing trim"
  width="300"
  style="image-rendering: pixelated; image-rendering: crisp-edges;"
/>
```

Do not apply pixelated rendering to screenshots, diagrams, or photographs, and
avoid raw `<img>` elements in article content because they bypass the shared
image treatment.

## Adding the Page to Navigation

Creating a route does not automatically add it to the sidebar. Wiki and guide
navigation is versioned, so edit the appropriate files:

```
src/lib/sidebar/tabs/latest/WikiPages.svelte
src/lib/sidebar/tabs/latest/Guides.svelte
src/lib/sidebar/tabs/pre-1_21_11/WikiPages.svelte
src/lib/sidebar/tabs/pre-1_21_11/Guides.svelte
```

Add a page to both version trees when it applies to both. If a feature only
exists in the latest version, add it only to the latest tree. Contribution
pages are shared and are added directly in `src/lib/sidebar/Sidebar.svelte`.

Import an icon from [Tabler Icons](https://tabler.io/icons), then add a
`SidebarPage` to the appropriate category:

```md
import IconExample from "~icons/tabler/file-description";

<SidebarCategory
  name="Concepts"
  icon={IconConcepts}
  activePath="/wiki/concepts"
>
  <SidebarPage
    label="Example Page"
    icon={IconExample}
    page="/wiki/concepts/example-page"
  />
</SidebarCategory>
```

The category's `activePath` keeps it open while one of its pages is selected
and highlights its icon when the sidebar is collapsed. It can be one route
prefix or an array when a category contains unrelated routes:

```md
activePath={["/guide/installing-a-datapack", "/guide/choosing-a-server"]}
```

If you are not sure where a page belongs, mention that in your pull request and
a maintainer can place it.

## Writing Guidelines

Write for readers who may be new to datapacking or who may not speak English as
their first language.

- Define unfamiliar terms or link to a page that explains them.
- Use concrete examples rather than describing a concept only in the abstract.
- Make pages easy to scan with headings, short paragraphs, lists, and useful
  whitespace.
- Prefer active voice. For example, write “The command teleports the pig”
  instead of “The pig is teleported by the command.”
- Explicitly name the subject instead of relying on vague words such as “it”.
- Avoid words such as _obvious_, _simple_, _basic_, _easy_, and _just_ when
  they could dismiss a reader's difficulty.
- Use the [Oxford comma](https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_comma).
- Check spelling and grammar before submitting the page.

Use bold and italics sparingly. Put commands, identifiers, file paths, and
short code values in inline code. Use tables only for genuinely tabular data.

## Formatting Reference

The site uses MDsveX, which combines Markdown with Svelte components. Most
pages need only the following features.

### Admonitions

Use an admonition for supporting information that should stand apart from the
main explanation. Available types are `note`, `tip`, `warning`, and `info`.

````md
:::info

Supporting information goes here.

:::
````

Do not use an admonition for every paragraph; it should remain a meaningful
visual interruption.

### Code Blocks and Titles

Fenced code blocks support syntax highlighting for `mcfunction`, `json`, `md`,
and `javascript`. Add a filename after the language to give the block a title:

````md
```json:pack.mcmeta
{
  "pack": {
    "pack_format": 107.1,
    "description": "Example datapack"
  }
}
```
````

Use an unlabelled code fence if the language is not supported. Keep examples
focused and explain the important lines in the surrounding text.

### Highlighted Text

The `Highlight` component can associate a short label with a colour when the
colour itself carries meaning:

<Highlight color="#DC2626">Website</Highlight>

```md
<script lang="ts">
  import { Highlight } from "$lib/reusables";
</script>

<Highlight color="#DC2626">Website</Highlight>
```

Use it sparingly and never rely on colour alone to communicate information.

For standard command notation, follow the examples on the
[Commands page](/wiki/concepts/commands).

## Before Opening a Pull Request

Check the page in the site, including at a narrow screen width, then run:

```
npx prettier --write path/to/changed/files
git diff --check
npm run check
npm run build
```

Before submitting, confirm that:

- the route, title, heading, and sidebar label agree;
- internal links and heading links work;
- local image paths use the correct filename and letter case;
- every informative image has useful alt text;
- code blocks do not overflow or hide important content; and
- the page is readable on both desktop and mobile.

Do not manually edit generated search files. If the search index needs to be
regenerated, leave that step to the current maintainer workflow.

Need help with Markdown, images, or deciding where a page belongs? Ask in the
[Datapack Hub Discord](https://discord.datapackhub.net) or describe the issue in
your pull request.
